{
  "source": {
    "title": "Edo numbers 1-100",
    "note": "1-10 from a printed numbers chart; 11-30 from photos of handwritten notes; 31-100 from a typed list. All supplied by the project owner, who confirmed 1 = owo / ọkpa.",
    "language": "Edo (Bini), Niger-Congo; main language of the ancient Kingdom of Benin",
    "transcription_caveats": [
      "11-30 are handwritten; the second form of 15 is read as 'ekesugie'.",
      "23 and 24 were written with ditto marks and are expanded.",
      "Spelling chosen by the project owner: 4 = 'enen', 5 = 'isèn', and 'yan' (plain y) for 'and', used in every number written as separate words.",
      "Kept as supplied: the one-word forms for 16 and 26 ('enẹirrọvbugie', 'enẹirrọvbọgban'), and 'iyisen' (100) and 'ekigbesiyisen' (90), which contain 5 inside a single word.",
      "36 and above write count-down numbers as separate words ('enen ẹi rrọ vbe iyeva'); the handwritten 16-19 and 26-29 run them together.",
      "'fourty' in the typed list is corrected to 'forty'.",
      "Most forms have no tone marks."
    ]
  },

